---
import BlogPost from '../../layouts/BlogPost.astro';
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import heroImg from '../../assets/attractions/funtown.jpg';
import excaliburImg from '../../assets/attractions/funtown-excalibur.jpg';
import oobBeachImg from '../../assets/oob/oob-beach.jpg';
import lobsterRollImg from '../../assets/travel/lobster-roll.jpg';
---

<BlogPost
	title="Funtown Splashtown USA: Tips, Best Rides & How to Plan Your Day"
	description="Maine's largest amusement and water park is less than 2 miles from the hotel. A full guide to Excalibur, the water slides, the new Lazy Lobster Lagoon, and how to plan a full day."
	date="2026-07-01"
	category="Attraction Guide"
	slug="funtown-splashtown-guide"
	heroImage={heroImg}
>
	<p>Funtown Splashtown USA has been on Route 1 in Saco since 1959. Family-owned, family-run, and still the same family. About 50 rides, water slides, pools, and attractions on one property, 1.5 miles from our <a href="/location">front door</a>. For mixed-age groups, it's the most complete park in southern Maine. Palace Playland has the beachfront and the atmosphere. Funtown has the full-day programming for everyone from toddlers to adults.</p>

	<p>We send a lot of guests here. Here's what actually matters.</p>

	<div class="update-note">
		<strong class="note-label">Updated October 2026</strong>
		<p>Funtown Splashtown closed for the 2026 season on September 8. Season passes for 2027 are already on sale. Opening dates for 2027 haven't been announced, but the park has traditionally opened Memorial Day weekend. Visiting this fall instead? See our <a href="/blog/fall-winter-saco-old-orchard-beach">fall and winter guide</a> for what's open nearby.</p>
	</div>

	<h2>Excalibur: The Reason Coaster People Come</h2>

	<figure>
		<Image src={excaliburImg} alt="Excalibur wooden roller coaster at Funtown Splashtown USA in Saco, Maine" loading="lazy" width={816} height={1089} />
		<figcaption>Excalibur opened in 1998. Maine's only wooden roller coaster, and still the largest coaster in state history.</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<p>Excalibur opened in 1998 and it's still the signature ride. Maine's only wooden roller coaster and the largest, tallest coaster in the state's history. It winds through the wooded edge of the park, and the trees make it feel longer than the stats suggest. People who care about wooden coasters drive from out of state specifically for this one. Best for ages 8 and up, minimum 48 inches to ride alone.</p>

	<h2>Astrosphere: The One Nobody Expects to Love</h2>

	<p>Opened in 1976. Changed very little since. A fully enclosed dome combining a Scrambler-style ride with a complete laser light show. The music is "Fire on High" by Electric Light Orchestra, playing on a loop. It sounds like the kind of thing you'd skip. Every guest we've talked to who actually rode it came back talking about it. It's been making people laugh and yell since before most of our guests were born. Do it.</p>

	<h2>Whispering Pines Haunted Hotel Dark Ride</h2>

	<p>The first new ride added to Funtown in two decades, opened in 2023. Custom vehicles through 14 rooms of a cursed hotel. It placed sixth in USA Today's 10Best Readers' Choice Awards for best new theme park attraction in the country. That's a national ranking against major parks. Worth at least one run.</p>

	<h2>Thunder Falls Log Flume</h2>

	<p>New England's longest and tallest log flume. You will get soaked. There's no version of this ride where you stay dry. Bring a change of clothes or accept that you're wet for the rest of the day. Most people make peace with the second option around 11 AM and it turns out fine.</p>

	<h2>The Water Park</h2>

	<p>Splashtown opens in mid to late June (the 2026 opening was June 20) and runs through Labor Day, roughly 11 AM to 5 PM. The highlights are Mammoth, Tornado, and Mount Olympus, the tallest and largest water slides of their kind in Maine. Amphitrite's Challenge is a four-lane mat racer where you can go side-by-side with your group.</p>

	<h3>New in 2026: Lazy Lobster Lagoon</h3>

	<p>For its 30th-anniversary season, Splashtown added the <strong>Lazy Lobster Lagoon</strong>, a 3,200-square-foot splash pad with zero-depth water. Nobody is ever in water over their ankles, so it's safe for every age, toddlers included. It's a good base for families with little ones while older kids run the big slides.</p>

	<p>On a hot August day some people spend their entire visit in the water park and never touch the amusement side. That is a completely legitimate strategy. The water park alone justifies the price of admission on a 90-degree day.</p>

	<h2>Hours and Season</h2>

	<p>Funtown (amusement park) opens Memorial Day weekend. In 2026 that was May 23, with hours of 11 AM to 5 PM early in the season and 11 AM to 6 PM once summer started. Splashtown (water park) opened June 20, 11 AM to 5 PM. Both ran daily through Labor Day, and the park closed for the season on September 8. There were three 21-plus nights in 2026 if you want to come back without the kids. Current hours are on the <a href="https://funtownsplashtownusa.com/park-hours/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Funtown park hours page</a>.</p>

	<h2>Pricing</h2>

	<p>2026 one-day tickets for both parks: Big Combo (48 inches and up) $62, Little Combo (38 to 48 inches) $49, Senior Combo (60 and up) $49. Arriving after 3 PM drops those to $39, $34, and $34. Ride-park-only and water-park-only tickets run a little less. Children under 38 inches are free.</p>

	<p>2027 season passes are on sale now: Big Combo $199.95, Little Combo $184.95, Senior Combo $131.95, and a Family & Friend 4-Pack at $759.95, with small discounts for 2026 pass holders who renew. If you're planning more than three visits next summer, the pass pays for itself. Check <a href="https://funtownsplashtownusa.com/rates-2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">funtownsplashtownusa.com</a> for current rates before your visit.</p>

	<h2>Height Guide</h2>

	<p>Under 38 inches: free admission, kiddie rides and the water park kiddie area. 38 to 48 inches: full kids section, log flume, water park, but not Excalibur solo. 48 inches and up: everything, including Excalibur.</p>

	<h2>Making the Most of the Day</h2>

	<figure>
		<Image src={oobBeachImg} alt="Old Orchard Beach, about 15 minutes from Funtown Splashtown" loading="lazy" width={1600} height={900} />
		<figcaption>Old Orchard Beach is about 15 minutes from Funtown. A common move is to do the park in the morning and head to OOB in the afternoon.</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<p>Get there when it opens (11 AM in 2026). Excalibur and Whispering Pines draw long lines by midday. Getting both done in the first hour changes the whole day.</p>

	<p>Weekdays, especially Tuesday and Wednesday in July, are noticeably less crowded than weekends. If your schedule has any give, mid-week is significantly better.</p>

	<p>Bring a change of clothes. Thunder Falls will soak you, and if you're doing the water park that's a second round of wet clothes to manage. A dry bag in the car is genuinely useful.</p>

	<p>And seriously, ride the Astrosphere. Everyone thinks it sounds cheesy. Everyone comes out smiling.</p>

	<h2>Getting There</h2>

	<p>774 Portland Road (Route 1), Saco, ME 04072. Less than a five-minute drive from the hotel going south on Route 1. Free parking on site. No OOB-style parking scramble. Check our <a href="/rooms">rooms</a> or <a href="/book">book direct</a> for the best rate. Want a quieter morning before the park? <a href="/blog/ferry-beach-state-park">Ferry Beach State Park</a> is about five minutes away.</p>

	<figure>
		<Image src={lobsterRollImg} alt="Maine lobster roll, a good lunch stop after Funtown Splashtown" loading="lazy" width={1200} height={800} />
		<figcaption>After a day at Funtown, downtown Biddeford is ten minutes away and has some of the best restaurants in Maine.</figcaption>
	</figure>

	<div class="tip-box">
		<strong>Two Parks, One Day</strong>
		<p>Funtown opened at 11 AM in 2026. <a href="/blog/palace-playland-guide">Palace Playland</a> at OOB opens at 11 AM on weekends and noon on weekdays. A lot of guests do Funtown in the morning, grab lunch, and drive to Old Orchard Beach for Palace Playland in the afternoon. About 15 minutes between the two parks. It's a long day but it works. Both parks are open through Labor Day.</p>
	</div>
</BlogPost>
